Unix utility 'protect'

The Unix utility 'protect' marks a path as protected, so that only the root user can remove,
rename or write to it. Protection is recorded on the volume itself, through the SYSTEM
attribute of FAT16B, and survives a reboot. Only the root user can run this utility.

With the -u parameter, the protection is removed from the given path instead. With -s, the
path is also marked setuid, which grants root to whoever runs it, no matter which user
started it. This is what allows 'su' and 'sudo' to do their work from a common user session.

With the -a parameter, every path listed in /etc/protect is protected in one pass. That file
holds one path per line, and a path may be followed by a space and the word setuid to also
receive the treatment -s gives. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored. This is
how the system protects itself at boot, from a line in /etc/rc.

USAGE:

protect <path>
protect -u <path>
protect -s <path>
protect -a

Example of use:

protect -s /sbin/su

It protects /sbin/su and marks it setuid, so a common user running it gets root.

For more information, enter 'protect ?' or 'protect --help'.

